      asKSeason: 'In India there are three main seasons. Kharif crops (cotton, maize, groundnut, rice) are sown with the monsoon in June to July. Rabi crops (wheat, gram, mustard) are sown after the monsoon in October to December. Zaid crops (vegetables, melons) are grown in the dry summer months with irrigation. Exact dates depend on the crop, the variety and your area, so check the local advice.',
      asKSoil: 'Healthy soil gives better yields. Test your soil every 2 to 3 years (a Soil Health Card test is available through the agriculture department) and fertilise by the result. Add compost or farmyard manure every year, keep the soil covered with crop residue or mulch, and avoid leaving fields bare.',
      asKComp: 'Compost, vermicompost and farmyard manure feed the soil and help it hold water. Pile crop residue, leaves and dung, keep the heap damp and turn it now and then; it is ready in about 2 to 3 months. Green manure crops such as dhaincha or sunnhemp, ploughed in before flowering, also add nitrogen and organic matter.',
